<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carthago Nova</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 227 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Cartagena, New Carthage, Qart Hadasht</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carthago Nova, modern Cartagena in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, was founded around 227 BCE by the Carthaginian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-the-fair">Hasdrubal the Fair</a> as the capital of Barcid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, with a fine harbour and nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> captured it in 209 BCE. It became a leading Roman city, the capital of the province of Carthaginiensis, and later a Byzantine stronghold.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carthago-nova.jpg" alt="Carthago Nova" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nanosanchez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_romano_cartagena.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The city may have been built on an earlier Iberian or Tartessian settlement, and Cartagena has traditionally been identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mastia">Mastia</a>, named in the Ora maritima of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avienus">Avienus</a> and in the second treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> of 348 BCE. The coast of Cartagena and Mazarrón was coveted for its lead, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and zinc, worked since Phoenician times, and its waters gave fish for salting. Strongly walled, the city was the main market for goods arriving by sea and for inland produce sent overseas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamilcar-barca">Hamilcar Barca</a>, seeking a dominion for his family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>, made the region the centre of his operations, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcid-family">Barcid family</a> the city, Qart Hadasht, became the chief Carthaginian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>. From there <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> in 218 BCE. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> took it, it was a tributary community, received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rights under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and became the colony Colonia Vrbs Iulia Nova Carthago in 44 BCE. Around 40,000 enslaved people are said to have worked in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and lead mines.</p><p>Under Augustus the city gained a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and forum. From the 2nd century it declined, perhaps as the mines were exhausted, and its eastern quarter was abandoned until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> made it a provincial capital in 298, when buildings rose again from Augustan materials and trade turned to the fish sauce garum. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> plundered it in the 5th century, and in 461 Emperor Majorian's fleet of 45 ships gathered there was destroyed at the Battle of Cartagena. After a time under Visigothic control, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> made it the capital of the Byzantine province of Spania, renamed Carthago Spartaria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Remains of the Roman city stand within modern Cartagena: the Republican amphitheatre beneath the bullring, the Augusteum and forum, the Casa de la Fortuna with its wall paintings and mosaics, and the Roman theatre, discovered in October 1988 and one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Roman Hispania</a>. Finds are shown in the Municipal Archaeological Museum, the National Museum of Underwater Archaeology and the Roman Theatre Museum, opened in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carthago Nova” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The newly conquered lands of Gallaecia, Asturia and Cantabria were attached to Citerior, probably during Augustus' visit of 16–13 BCE, creating a vast province that stretched from the Pyrenees and the Mediterranean coast to the Atlantic shores of Galicia and south to <b>Carthago Nova</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They took wine and olives from the Greeks, horse breeding mattered greatly to the nobility, and they worked the silver mines near Gadir and <b>Carthago Nova</b>, iron in the Ebro valley, and tin and copper.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in about 229 BCE, his son-in-law Hasdrubal extended Carthaginian power by diplomacy and founded <b>Qart Hadasht</b>, later Carthago Nova, around 227 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a></li><li><blockquote>Spania was run by a magister militum based at <b>Carthago Nova</b>, and an inscription of 589 records the governor Comenciolus repairing the city's defences.</blockquote><a href="/state/byzantine-spania">Byzantine Spania</a></li><li><blockquote>It took in the south-east coast and much of the central Meseta, including Toletum, with <b>Carthago Nova</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/carthaginiensis">Carthaginiensis</a></li><li><blockquote>After Carthaginian control under Barcid Iberia, the region passed to Rome when Scipio captured <b>Carthago Nova</b> in 209 BCE, and was pacified by 197 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/contestani">Contestani</a></li></ul></section>
